BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-horizons-parma-360-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Horizons: Art Beyond Algorithms (PARMA 360 Festival\, 
 Parma European Youth Capital 2027)
DESCRIPTION:First edition of an international call for artists under 35 wor
 king with artificial intelligence\, digital languages and emerging technol
 ogies\, run by 360° Creativity Events APS for PARMA 360 Festival and Parm
 a European Youth Capital 2027. It follows the 2026 pilot exhibition SYNTHE
 TIC HORIZONS (curated by Chiara Canali). The call focuses on open-source a
 nd closed-source image and video generation tools and their creative\, soc
 ial and cultural impact. THEMES: Parma Heritage (crafts\, traditions\, arc
 hives\, immersive storytelling of the city)\; La Cura / Care (wellbeing\, 
 accessibility\, AI ethics\, health tech\, public space)\; Sustainability a
 nd Data Ecology (energy and water cost of AI\, data extraction and digital
  colonialism\, waste of digital images\, ethics of automated production). 
 ACCEPTED FORMATS: digital and synthetic images\, NFTs\, printed digital ph
 otography\, digital/3D-printed sculpture\, video\, audio\, interactive bro
 wser or chatbot installations\, software art\, VR\, AR\, sound art\, perfo
 rmance and hybrids. Works must already be completed but need not be unpubl
 ished. One application per artist. REQUIRED: an Artist Statement (max 1\,5
 00 characters) on how you use AI\, naming software\, models and workflow\;
  missing statements are excluded. SELECTION: a jury of the PARMA 360 artis
 tic direction\, the Parma EYC27 committee and AI/new-tech experts picks 20
  finalists (at least 3 from the Parma area)\, announced from 1 December 20
 26\; finalists show in a group exhibition curated by Chiara Canali in Parm
 a in April-May 2027. The organiser covers shipping to the show and AV equi
 pment hire and gives finalists one hotel night at the opening\; return shi
 pping and travel are at the artist's cost. Prize-winning works are acquire
 d in full or in part. AGE: must not have turned 35 by the closing date. DE
 ADLINE: 31 October 2026\, midnight Rome time\, via the Google Form linked 
 in the call. Questions: aihorizonscall@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: 360° C
 reativity Events APS (PARMA 360 Festival della creatività contemporanea)\
 , with Parma European Youth Capital 2027\nLocation: Parma\, Italy. Open to
  Italian and international artists under 35 and students of the Accademie 
 di Belle Arti. Finalists exhibit in Parma in April-May 2027.\nAward: EUR 6
 \,000 in cash prizes (gross): 1st AI Horizons Prize EUR 1\,500 (overall wi
 nner) plus three sponsor prizes of EUR 1\,500 each (Polo Parma for Parma H
 eritage\, COLSER-Auroradomus for La Cura\, DS Solutions for Sustainability
  and Data Ecology). The top prize can be combined with a sponsor prize\, s
 o one artist can receive up to EUR 3\,000. Prizes are treated as full or p
 artial acquisition of the work. Non-cash: Piazza of Beauty residency in Pa
 rma during the show\, plus Rome New Media Week 2027 and Premio Giovanarte 
 exhibition mentions. Free to enter.\nDuration: Finalist group exhibition i
 n April-May 2027 (11th PARMA 360 Festival)\n\nMore: https://forms.gle/ymoW
 bcjuDyzJY11P6?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://forms.gle/ymoWbcjuDyzJY11P6?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Parma\, Italy. Open to Italian and international artists under 35 
 and students of the Accademie di Belle Arti. Finalists exhibit in Parma in
  April-May 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gainssa-ai-safety-essentials-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261109
SUMMARY:Deadline: GAIN SSA: AI Safety Essentials Fellowship (inaugural coho
 rt)
DESCRIPTION:GAIN SSA (Global AI Youth Network\, Sub-Saharan Africa) runs th
 e AI Safety Essentials Fellowship for residents of Africa considering how 
 they can be effective contributors in shaping the continent's transition t
 oward containing risks from transformative AI. Fellows spend six weeks map
 ping the priorities and gaps that need to be filled in Africa's AI safety 
 ecosystem\, through curated pre-reading and a live group call each week (a
 bout 5 hours per week). CURRICULUM: existential risks and catastrophic vec
 tors\; frontier AI models and Africa\; defining safe and beneficial AI\; s
 ubfields and alignment architectures\; Africa's AI safety landscape and po
 licy\; and a capstone week on fellowships and career launch\, with guidanc
 e toward international pipelines such as MATS\, ERA\, BlueDot and GovAI. M
 entorship comes from international AI safety researchers\, governance fell
 ows and peer builders. FUNDING: after the program\, top capstone action-pl
 an proposals are funded from a USD 500 to 1\,000 micro-grant pool (capped 
 at USD 1\,000)\, including proposals needing compute\, travel to AI alignm
 ent conferences in Sub-Saharan Africa\, or communication work (for example
  Substack Pro for writers)\; results are shared within 10 days of receivin
 g a proposal. The main aim is to have participants take action to build Af
 rica's first AI safety systems. APPLICATION: expression of interest for th
 e inaugural cohort via the Google Form linked from the program page. DEADL
 INE: expressions of interest close 8 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: GAIN 
 SSA (Global AI Youth Network\, Sub-Saharan Africa)\nLocation: Online (virt
 ual\, facilitated). Open to residents of African countries.\nAward: Micro-
 grant pool funding top capstone proposals at USD 500 to 1\,000 each (cappe
 d at USD 1\,000)\, usable for compute access\, communication work such as 
 Substack Pro subscriptions for writers\, or travel to AI alignment confere
 nces in Sub-Saharan Africa. The fellowship itself is free to attend (no pa
 rticipation fee\, no application fee)\; no stipend is paid during the prog
 ram.\nDuration: 6-week facilitated virtual curriculum plus an onboarding p
 re-requisites module (the site also describes it as 7 weeks)\, about 5 hou
 rs per week with one live group call per week\; capstone proposals are fun
 ded afterwards\, with results within 10 days of submission.\n\nMore: https
 ://gainssa.org/initiatives/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://gainssa.org/initiatives/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online (virtual\, facilitated). Open to residents of African count
 ries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:culture360-asef-arts-in-the-wake-of-ai-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260929
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:Deadline: culture360 / ASEF: Call for Content\, Arts in the Wake of
  AI
DESCRIPTION:culture360.ASEF.org\, the arts platform of the Asia-Europe Foun
 dation\, invites proposals from writers and content producers for its open
  call 'Arts in the Wake of AI': articles\, podcasts and video interviews t
 hat foreground how cultural professionals and organisations across Asia an
 d Europe are adapting to AI technologies\, from generative tools and machi
 ne learning to recommender systems and algorithms. WHAT THEY WANT: case-st
 udy-based contributions grounded in real-world evidence\, experiences or p
 ractices\, on topics including the impact of AI on cultural diversity\, fa
 irness and inclusivity\; the environmental costs of AI in the cultural sec
 tor\; and AI governance in the cultural sector (policy\, copyright\, intel
 lectual property and the ethical use of AI-generated cultural content). Ac
 ademic research papers and non-arts subjects are not considered. FEE: SGD 
 250 per commissioned article\, podcast or video interview. FORMATS: journa
 listic articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four credited images\
 ; podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes. ELIGIBILITY: national
 s of ASEM partner countries with previous experience in writing\, podcasti
 ng or video production\, proficiency in English\, and knowledge of or invo
 lvement in the Asian and European arts and culture scene\; an existing cha
 nnel for further dissemination is an advantage\; the work is fully virtual
 . Up to two proposals per application. Only selected writers are notified.
  Queries to culture[at]asef.org. Deadline 29 September 2026 at 6 PM Singap
 ore time\, via the online application form.\n\nOrganisation: Asia-Europe F
 oundation (ASEF)\, culture360.ASEF.org\nLocation: Fully remote\; contribut
 ors do not need to be based in Singapore. Open to nationals of ASEM partne
 r countries across Asia and Europe.\nAward: SGD 250 fee per commissioned a
 rticle\, podcast or video interview. Up to two proposals per application\,
  of which at most one is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: One-off 
 editorial commissions: articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four 
 credited pictures\, podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes\n\nM
 ore: https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully remote\; contributors do not need to be based in Singapore. 
 Open to nationals of ASEM partner countries across Asia and Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iaps-ai-policy-fellowship-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: IAPS: AI Policy Fellowship Spring 2027 (DC\, London or re
 mote)
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS) runs a three-mo
 nth\, fully funded AI policy fellowship placing fellows on concrete AI gov
 ernance and policy research projects. Spring 2027 cohort: 22 February to 1
 4 May 2027\, opening with a mandatory in-person component in Washington DC
  from 22 February to 5 March 2027\, after which fellows work from the DC o
 ffice\, the London office or remotely. STIPENDS: USD 18\,000 for Fellows a
 nd USD 24\,000 for Senior Fellows\, plus USD 3\,000 extra on the in-person
  DC or London tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional deve
 lopment stipend. ELIGIBILITY: no technical background or prior policy expe
 rience required\; the DC track requires the right to work in the US betwee
 n 22 February and 14 May 2027\, and no visa sponsorship is available for t
 he US or UK\; the remote track is open globally with country-specific exce
 ptions. TIMELINE: initial responses mid-October 2026\, live interviews in 
 November\, final selections early December. Deadline 27 September 2026. Ap
 ply at https://www.guidedtrack.com/programs/ll4vpg7/run.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS)\nLocation: Washington DC or Lo
 ndon track (desk space provided)\, or fully remote from most countries. Ma
 ndatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22 February to 5 March 2027. 
 No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.\nAward: Fellows receive USD 18\,000 
 and Senior Fellows USD 24\,000 as a stipend for the three-month full-time 
 program\, plus an additional USD 3\,000 stipend on the in-person DC or Lon
 don tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional development st
 ipend. Up to 45 fellows. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months full-
 time\, 22 February to 14 May 2027\; extensions of 4 to 12 weeks were offer
 ed to more than half of the previous cohort\n\nMore: https://www.iaps.ai/f
 ellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iaps.ai/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC or London track (desk space provided)\, or fully rem
 ote from most countries. Mandatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22
  February to 5 March 2027. No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:humanity-ai-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Humanity AI: Open Call for Proposals 2026
DESCRIPTION:Humanity AI\, an initiative of ten leading philanthropies\, ope
 ns a USD 10 million call for community-led initiatives that shape how AI a
 ffects people's lives. ISSUE AREAS: Education (student and educator-center
 ed work shaping AI's educational impact)\; Labor and Economy (worker-led e
 fforts to ensure AI enhances rather than replaces employment)\; and Humani
 ties\, Arts\, and Culture (artist and creator-focused work protecting cult
 ural contributions). ELIGIBLE APPROACHES: shaping the rules for AI governa
 nce and deployment\; building AI controlled by communities rather than cor
 porations\; and challenging misaligned AI deployments. Community-led means
  the affected community defines the goals and shapes the approach. Priorit
 y goes to open-weight and open-source AI models over proprietary ones. OUT
  OF SCOPE: AI literacy training\, AI adoption promotion\, and research-onl
 y work. GRANTS: three tiers from USD 75\,000 to 1\,000\,000\, up to three 
 years. ELIGIBILITY: US-based 501(c)(3) organizations as lead applicants\; 
 individuals via fiscal sponsors or collaborations\; Tribal governments tre
 ated as States under IRC Section 7871\; for-profit entities only as collab
 orators\, not leads\; all proposals must serve US-based communities. TIMEL
 INE: launched 10 September 2026\; applicant webinar 14 September 2026\; in
 itial applications due 21 October 2026 at 4:59pm PT\; finalists notified D
 ecember 2026\; awards announced early spring 2027. Apply via Submittable\;
  an organizational readiness tool on the site helps assess eligibility fir
 st.\n\nOrganisation: Humanity AI (coalition of 10 philanthropies)\nLocatio
 n: United States. Lead applicants must be US-based 501(c)(3) organizations
  (or Tribal governments)\; all proposals must serve US-based communities. 
 Individuals can apply through a fiscal sponsor or as part of a collaborati
 on.\nAward: USD 10 million total pool\, grants of USD 75\,000 to 1\,000\,0
 00 across three tiers: Tier 1 USD 75\,000-150\,000\; Tier 2 USD 150\,000-5
 00\,000\; Tier 3 USD 500\,000-1\,000\,000. Grants run up to three years. N
 o application fee.\nDuration: Up to three years\n\nMore: https://humanitya
 i.ai/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://humanityai.ai/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. Lead applicants must be US-based 501(c)(3) organiza
 tions (or Tribal governments)\; all proposals must serve US-based communit
 ies. Individuals can apply through a fiscal sponsor or as part of a collab
 oration.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:darc-transmediale-interstices-research-workshop-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x DARC: Interstices\, Research (in) the Gaps
 \, PhD/Research Workshop
DESCRIPTION:The annual DARC PhD/Research Workshop\, organised by the Digita
 l Aesthetics Research Center at Aarhus University with transmediale\, resp
 onds to transmediale's 2027 thematic framework. THEME: 'Interstices: Resea
 rch (in) the gaps'. An interstice is the small space between objects set c
 losely together\, the cords in a rope or the atoms in a crystal lattice: a
 n emptiness produced by the structure it belongs to\, and without which th
 at structure could not be what it is. In crystallography\, interstitial si
 tes are the spaces a lattice and its basis generate but do not fill\, and 
 interstitial diffusion\, the movement of small atoms through the gaps of a
  host lattice\, is the mechanism by which materials transform. The call as
 ks what the equivalent sites are in social\, cultural and computational st
 ructures: where are the gaps these combinations generate but do not fill\,
  what moves through them\, and do they carry transformative power? It work
 s through Yancey Strickler's dark forest theory of the internet (the withd
 rawal of meaningful exchange from the open\, surveilled\, indexed web into
  private channels)\, the Dark Forest Collective's anthology and its 'cozyw
 eb' of small sheltered spaces\, dark nets\, miniverses and moving castles 
 as interstitial spaces produced by and defined against the dominant web\, 
 and Bogna Konior's extension of the dark forest metaphor to the opacity of
  AI systems\, where silence is refusal rather than absence and the forest 
 is increasingly inhabited by other-than-human intelligences that opt out o
 f engagement with people. Where big tech pursues efficiency through the au
 tomation of human perception\, cognition\, identification and aesthetics\,
  artists and researchers explore the non/more-than-human\, glitchy\, noisy
 \, machinic\, datafied and dark to build new expression and critique of da
 ta-ism and what is marketed as artificial intelligence\, including the dan
 gers and potentials inside badly curated data models\, between polluting d
 ata centres\, and in skeuomorphic interfaces. WHAT THEY WANT: proposals fr
 om artists\, researchers and practitioners for work that occupies and move
 s through gaps that are social as well as structural\, ontological and mat
 erial. The gaps are physical too: the heat of data centres and their thirs
 t for water\, latency in the cables\, unindexed nodes. The call invites sp
 eculations\, practical interventions and inferences that fill the gaps and
  expand the diversity of possible communications and communities that can 
 inhabit dark forests\, including those built on\, against and beneath the 
 infrastructures underlying them\, and welcomes work on the (dis-)continuou
 s histories of technological exploration of computational infrastructures 
 over the last 30 years or longer\, and on how current artistic\, theoretic
 al and technical explorations repeat or break from them. WHO: PhD candidat
 es and early-career researchers\, with submissions welcome from researcher
 s inside and outside the academy\, including artists\, designers and curat
 ors. PARTICIPANT EXPECTATIONS: participants write a paper and present it a
 t the workshop\, and on the basis of that peer review shorten the research
  into a newspaper-length article for distribution at the transmediale fest
 ival. The workshop is led by scholars from DARC at Aarhus University. DEAD
 LINE: 28 August 2026\, 23:59 CEST\, via the application link on the call p
 age. Queries to Pablo Velasco\, pvelasco[at]cc.au.dk.\n\nOrganisation: Dig
 ital Aesthetics Research Center (DARC)\, Aarhus University\, in collaborat
 ion with transmediale\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alon
 gside transmediale 2027 (festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to research
 ers based anywhere\, inside or outside the academy.\nAward: No submission 
 fee. The published call does not state a participation fee\, honorarium\, 
 travel or accommodation support\, so confirm with the organisers before bu
 dgeting. Accepted participants present a paper at the workshop and their c
 ondensed text is distributed as a newspaper-style article at the festival.
 \nDuration: Research workshop held around transmediale 2027 in Berlin (fes
 tival 26 to 31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/open
 -call-for-darc-phd-research-workshop-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/open-call-for-darc-phd-research-worksho
 p-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alongside transmediale 2027 (
 festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to researchers based anywhere\, insi
 de or outside the academy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inato-gallery-touch-some-grass-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inato Gallery: Touch Some Grass (open-call poster exhibit
 ion)
DESCRIPTION:Touch Some Grass is an open-call poster exhibition responding t
 o the growing presence of AI-generated imagery and its impact on creative 
 labour\, authorship and visual culture. At a time when images can be produ
 ced instantly and consumed just as quickly\, the exhibition reclaims the p
 oster as a space for ideas\, intention and public conversation\, inviting 
 artists to make work that questions\, challenges or reflects on this chang
 ing landscape. As the organisers put it\, the question is no longer whethe
 r machines can make images\, they can\; the question is what kind of world
  we build when speed matters more than thought. NOTE: this call offers exh
 ibition and visibility only\, with no artist fee or stipend. SUBMISSION GU
 IDELINES: artwork size A3 (297 x 420 mm)\, portrait orientation\, 300 DPI\
 , CMYK recommended\, print-quality PDF preferred\, maximum file size 1 GB\
 , one submission per artist. CAP: participation is limited to 60 artists a
 nd the submission form closes automatically once all slots are filled\, so
  the effective deadline may fall well before 31 August 2026 (10 of 60 slot
 s were taken at the time of listing). Inato Gallery is an artist-run space
  in Iligan City\, Philippines\, which began as a solo street exhibition by
  KaraTula and now runs concept-driven solo and group shows\, open mics\, t
 alks and workshops built on openness\, participation and the belief that a
 rt should exist where people already are.\n\nOrganisation: Inato Gallery\n
 Location: Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or tr
 avel is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-
 run space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.\nAward: No artist fee\, stipend or production budget. This is an exhib
 ition opportunity: selected posters are shown in Inato Gallery's Touch Som
 e Grass exhibition. No submission fee is stated in the call.\nDuration: On
 e-off poster exhibition. Exhibition dates are not announced yet.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or trav
 el is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-ru
 n space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stated
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:european-ai-society-fund-building-narrative-power-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Building Narrative Power: funding and learning programme
DESCRIPTION:The European AI & Society Fund is launching Building Narrative 
 Power\, a EUR 2 million funding and learning programme to counter Big Tech
 's dominance over how AI is understood\, and to help people and communitie
 s shape new ways of thinking and talking about the technology they want. T
 he Fund's strategy is to build the power of the public interest community 
 to shape the direction of AI in Europe\; narratives are central to creatin
 g the conditions for a greater say over how AI is adopted and used. THREE 
 THEMES where research shows the public already feels strongly: (1) DATA CE
 NTRES: as the EU aims to triple data centre numbers in the next five to se
 ven years\, pollution\, energy prices and compromised grid access have mob
 ilised communities across the political spectrum\; proposals should explor
 e narrative questions that move communities beyond resistance towards enga
 gement in shaping data centre expansion. (2) AI AND JOBS: 57% of the publi
 c in a recent study expressed concern about how AI will change employment\
 , more pronounced among lower-income people\, and the concern is not only 
 displacement but the quality and nature of work\, autonomy and dignity\; p
 roposals should move the debate from generalised anxiety into a mobilising
  issue that shapes the future of work. (3) YOUNG PEOPLE: beyond the stride
 nt social media ban debate\, young people encounter AI in education\, frie
 ndships and future careers\, and their wider views are rarely heard\; prop
 osals should build narratives that help young people shape the technologie
 s that will influence their lives. LEARNING PROGRAMME: grantees join a com
 munity of practice\, sharing experiences across issues and places\, suppor
 ting each other through delivery and reflecting on which strategies and st
 ories have most salience\, with findings feeding back to the Fund and othe
 r funders. ABOUT THE FUNDER: the European AI & Society Fund is a pooled fu
 nd with 18 funding partners that mobilises a public interest community in 
 Europe to fight for AI that serves people\, society and planet\; it takes 
 no EU\, government or Big Tech money. ELIGIBILITY: this call is only open 
 to organisations in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, the Republic 
 of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Gre
 ece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithu
 ania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, the Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\,
  Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden and Switzerland\; applicat
 ions from the UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries ar
 e not accepted. Guidance for applicants and a full application-form previe
 w are published so you can prepare a draft before submitting. HOW TO APPLY
 : create an account on the Fund's application platform Optimy (link in the
  guidance documents) and submit the first-stage application before 31 Augu
 st 2026. KEY DATES: call launched 16 July 2026\; information sessions 22 J
 uly\, 29 July and 24 August 2026 (11:00 to 12:00 CET)\; first-stage deadli
 ne 31 August 2026\; unsuccessful applicants notified 16 October 2026\; sho
 rtlisted applicants invited to submit a detailed proposal on 19 October 20
 26\, due 16 November 2026\; funding decisions notified 14 December 2026\; 
 grants start February 2027. Questions to info@europeanaifund.org\; an FAQ 
 covering eligibility\, coalitions\, non-profit status and what the grant c
 an be spent on is published at the bottom of the programme page.\n\nOrgani
 sation: European AI & Society Fund\nLocation: Organisations registered in 
 Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, De
 nmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\
 , Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malt
 a\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Sloven
 ia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. The UK\, the Western Balkans and other
  non-European countries are not eligible for this programme.\nAward: Grant
 s of up to EUR 150\,000 from a EUR 2 million programme\, plus a place in t
 he accompanying learning programme and community of practice. Payments are
  usually 50% on grant signing\, 40% midway through the grant period subjec
 t to previous funds having been spent\, and 10% on completion. As a genera
 l rule organisations should not request more than their previous annual bu
 dget unless the need is motivated and demonstrated\; without a previous an
 nual budget\, request an amount proportional to the project. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Project grant with an accompanying learning programme\; 
 grants start February 2027\n\nMore: https://europeanaifund.org/newspublica
 tions/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-building-narrative-power-funding-and-lear
 ning-programme/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://europeanaifund.org/newspublications/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-
 building-narrative-power-funding-and-learning-programme/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Organisations registered in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia
 \, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, G
 ermany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtens
 tein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Po
 rtugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. Th
 e UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries are not eligib
 le for this programme.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-grain-cookbook-critical-ai-literacy-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN: Grain\, Open Call for Critical AI Literacy Rec
 ipes
DESCRIPTION:Grain is a cookbook of critical and creative AI literacy exerci
 ses for educators in film and the wider audiovisual and creative field\, c
 o-produced by AIxDESIGN and the Netherlands Film Academy and led by Nadia 
 Piet. It publishes in March/April 2027\, digital and print\, under CC BY-S
 A 4.0. The current open call is to propose a recipe by the end of August 2
 026 and join as a launch contributor. WHAT THEY WANT: tried-and-tested gro
 up exercises you have actually run with people in teaching or workshops\, 
 specifically in the context of film\, audiovisual work\, time-based media 
 and storytelling. Hands-on or analog\, 15 minutes or a full day\, beginner
  or advanced\; the one hard requirement is that another educator can read 
 your recipe and run it themselves. What that might look like: hands-on exp
 eriments with specific AI tools (image generators\, chatbots\, code assist
 ants\, video models)\; analog activities that reveal computational logic t
 hrough embodied practice\; critical readings\, watchings or listenings wit
 h structured discussion\; provocations\, games or role-plays that get at h
 ow AI sees\, thinks or makes\; and exercises that help people work out how
  they want to relate to AI in their own practice. WHAT MAKES A GOOD RECIPE
 : it helps a room of people think together and trusts the room to be smart
  given the right container\; it emerges from real practice rather than an 
 untested idea\; it is specific about what participants do\, step by step\,
  so a stranger could pick it up and adapt it to their group\, time\, tools
  or context\; it has at least one designed social moment where the thinkin
 g happens between people rather than privately\; it is about something lar
 ger than technique (how a machine sees an image\, where your values bump u
 p against what the tools want from you\, what a film's rhythm looks like l
 aid out in space instead of time)\; it takes a position without telling pe
 ople what to think\; and it points beyond itself with references\, reading
  and reference projects. Published example recipes include Image Forgery: 
 Archival Mimesis with AI (Artefacto)\, Machine Ways of Seeing (Nadia Piet)
 \, AI Film Now: A Close Watching Exercise (Nadia Piet) and Outside the Tim
 eline: Spatial Grid + Visual Clustering (Artefacto)\, alongside a root rec
 ipe template. WHAT LAUNCH CONTRIBUTORS GET: your recipe in the first editi
 on\, print and online\, credited to you\; a print copy when it lands and a
 n invite to the launch event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\; support turning
  your exercise into a recipe\, via Recipe Press (their LLM helper)\, the c
 ook-along\, or directly with the team\; and a need-based fee of EUR 150 pe
 r recipe for freelance and independent practitioners\, which you request i
 n the proposal form. After this round contributions stay open but web-only
 : no fee\, no dedicated support\, no print. COOK-ALONG: one live 30-minute
  online session on Wednesday 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST\, where you brin
 g a rough exercise and turn it into a recipe on the spot with Recipe Press
  (sign up at luma.com/84w148in). HOW IT WORKS: (1) propose your recipe thr
 ough the proposal form (who you are\, what the exercise is\, where you hav
 e run it\, and whether you are requesting the fee)\; a short pitch is enou
 gh\, and if it is already written you can just link your draft\; (2) they 
 reply on a rolling basis\, with one reply covering both whether it is a ma
 tch for the first edition and the fee if you requested it\, so the earlier
  you send the earlier you hear back\; (3) once it is a yes\, write it up u
 sing the structure on the Contribute a Recipe page\, with Recipe Press or 
 at the cook-along. TIMELINE: proposals open until the end of August 2026\,
  reviewed as they come in\; accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 
 2026\; Grain publishes March/April 2027. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\
 n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-produced with the Netherlands Film Academy
 \nLocation: Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anyw
 here\nAward: Need-based fee of EUR 150 per recipe for freelance and indepe
 ndent practitioners: say so in the proposal form if making the time would 
 be hard without it\, and it is settled in the same reply. Launch contribut
 ors also get their recipe in the first edition of Grain in print and onlin
 e\, credited to them\, a print copy when it lands\, an invite to the launc
 h event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\, and support turning the exercise int
 o a recipe. Published under CC BY-SA 4.0\; you keep authorship. No applica
 tion fee.\nDuration: One-off contribution: proposals by end of August 2026
 \, accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 2026\; Grain publishes Ma
 rch/April 2027\n\nMore: https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cook
 book-for-Critical-AI-Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cookbook-for-Critical-AI-
 Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anywhere
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-kb-national-library-ai-exhibition-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN x KB: Open Call for Sessions\, (Art)works and P
 erformances
DESCRIPTION:AIxDESIGN and the KB (Koninklijke Bibliotheek\, the National Li
 brary of the Netherlands) are building an exhibition and public programme 
 about AI\, made from the national collection\, at the KB in The Hague. Nad
 ia Piet is Creative Lead with the AIxDESIGN community\, co-presented with 
 the KB. The premise: long before neural nets\, libraries were the machines
  we built to organise human knowledge\, and the show uses the KB's collect
 ion to look at what AI is\, where it comes from\, and how it connects to t
 he older story of how we store\, sort\, and search what we know. Part of t
 he show lives in the KB Atelier\, with talks\, workshops\, and screenings 
 alongside\, intended as AI literacy you can wander into. WHAT THEY WANT: w
 ork that engages AI in some way (critical\, creative\, hands-on\, or playf
 ul)\; bonus if it also speaks to archives\, libraries\, or how we store an
 d sort knowledge\, though you do not need to touch the KB's collection to 
 take part. You may pitch to more than one track: (A) SOMETHING FOR THE OPE
 NING NIGHT\, a performance\, talk\, screening\, tour\, ritual\, booth\, ga
 me\, or other experience for the November 2026 opening\, fee EUR 1\,500 to
  2\,000\; (B) A SESSION IN THE EVENTS PROGRAMME\, a workshop or other sess
 ion once during the three-month run\, around 2.5 to 4 hours\, fee EUR 2\,0
 00 to 2\,500\; (C) AN (ART)WORK FOR THE EXHIBITION\, existing work in any 
 format (installation\, sculpture\, zine stand\, a corner of your own) stur
 dy enough for a public area with daily traffic\, honorarium EUR 200 to 400
  including licensing and production\; (D) SOMETHING ELSE ENTIRELY\, pitch 
 it anyway. WHO: artists\, designers\, researchers\, technologists\, perfor
 mers\, musicians\, archivists\, or educators working around AI\, data\, li
 braries\, or cultural heritage\; no finished idea or polished CV needed\, 
 just a clear\, specific pitch. You can be based anywhere and they especial
 ly welcome people outside the Netherlands\; the show is in-person in The H
 ague and the fee stays the same wherever you are coming from. HOW TO APPLY
 : send a short pitch through the form (which track\, your idea in a paragr
 aph or two\, roughly what it would need\, and a link to your work)\; takes
  about five minutes\, no full application required. Deadline: end of Augus
 t 2026. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-p
 resented with the Koninklijke Bibliotheek (KB)\, National Library of the N
 etherlands\nLocation: KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition a
 nd programme)\; pitches welcome from people based anywhere\nAward: Fees by
  track: opening-night experience EUR 1\,500 to 2\,000\; a session in the e
 vents programme EUR 2\,000 to 2\,500\; an existing (art)work for the exhib
 ition EUR 200 to 400 honorarium (including licensing and production). The 
 fee stays the same wherever you are based.\nDuration: Exhibition runs Nove
 mber 2026 to February 2027\; opening event November 2026\n\nMore: https://
 nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-works-More-38f
 37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-wor
 ks-More-38f37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition and programme)\
 ; pitches welcome from people based anywhere
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddc-trygfonden-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Digital Democracy Centre x TrygFonden Fellowship Program 
 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Digital Democracy Centre (DDC) at the University of Souther
 n Denmark\, with support from TrygFonden\, is calling for applications for
  its 2027 fellowship cohort. The program fosters collaboration between res
 earchers\, journalists\, and policymakers exploring the impact of digital 
 technology and AI on media\, politics\, and democracy. Fellows collaborate
  with a self-selected DDC mentor to generate new knowledge\, contribute to
  public debate\, develop networks\, and produce tangible outreach outputs 
 such as policy briefs\, educational packages\, and talks. THREE FELLOWSHIP
  TYPES: (1) academic fellows researching digital technologies and democrac
 y\; (2) journalist fellows covering digital technology and democracy\; (3)
  policy fellows contributing to the regulatory debate. Target group: resea
 rchers\, journalism graduates\, and policymakers in research/educational i
 nstitutions\, media organizations\, ministries\, NGOs\, etc. PRIORITIES: p
 rojects with clear relevance to Denmark or Danish society (including compa
 rative/international projects yielding insights relevant to a Danish conte
 xt)\; in 2027\, projects related to DDC's Hybrid Warfare Initiative (socie
 tal resilience to new forms of international conflict\, with a focus on di
 gital technologies and AI) are also prioritized. EXPECTATIONS: applicants 
 must secure confirmation of mentorship from their desired DDC mentor BEFOR
 E applying (applications without a confirmed mentor are automatically reje
 cted\; PhD students and research assistants are generally not eligible as 
 mentors\, and Sten Schaumburg-Muller is unavailable as a mentor for the 20
 27 call)\; applicants must also coordinate support from their current supe
 rvisors/managers. Fellows engage in DDC activities\, seminars/workshops\, 
 and public outreach\, and submit a short report within two months of finis
 hing. APPLICATION (in English): preliminary project title\, fellowship typ
 e\, brief project description (max 1000 chars)\, detailed description and 
 timeline (max 2400 chars\, including duration\, confirmed mentor\, and rem
 ote/in-office work plan)\, description of activities and collaboration wit
 h external practice partners (max 1000 chars)\, expected product/impact/ou
 tput (max 500 chars)\, a CV (max 5 pages)\, and an overview of the posts f
 or which financial support is sought. Submit the application form (PDF or 
 Word) plus CV as PDFs to ddc@sdu.dk with subject line 'DDC / TrygFonden Fe
 llowship application'. Deadline 1 September 2026\; decisions communicated 
 no later than 1 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Digital Democracy Centre (U
 niversity of Southern Denmark)\, with support from TrygFonden\nLocation: O
 dense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of in-offic
 e stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned to the Dan
 ish working calendar.\nAward: Either a salary subsidy or a stipend to help
  with travel and/or housing\; specific benefits (financial support\, acces
 s to DDC research resources\, flexible working spaces\, speaker series\, n
 etworks) vary by agreement between the fellow\, DDC\, and partnering organ
 izations. Applicants describe the posts they seek support for (flights\, h
 ousing\, research costs\, salary compensation\, stipend\, etc.).\nDuration
 : Flexible\; typically 2-6 months (minimum two months\, can include shorte
 r DDC stays and remote periods)\, longer in exceptional cases. Fellowships
  start anywhere in 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forsknin
 gsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracycentre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowshi
 p-call-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forskningsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracy
 centre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowship-call-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Odense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of
  in-office stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned t
 o the Danish working calendar.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:prototype-fund-switzerland-responsible-sustainable-ai-2026-27@artificia
 lnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Prototype Fund Switzerland 2026-27: Responsible and Susta
 inable AI
DESCRIPTION:Since 2020 the Prototype Fund Switzerland has strengthened the 
 common good with open-source\, public-interest technology\, supporting int
 erdisciplinary teams to develop and test early-stage projects that show ho
 w open source\, data and digital technologies such as AI can create real s
 ocietal value. The 2026-2027 edition focuses on Responsible and Sustainabl
 e AI: building the path toward a more responsible and sustainable AI\, tec
 h and digitalization landscape\, open source and beyond. THIS YEAR'S FOCUS
 : projects that reduce resource use\, energy consumption or emissions\; st
 rengthen accountability\, transparency and governance\; and contribute to 
 resilient and trustworthy digital systems\, including sustainable digitali
 zation and digital sufficiency\, public-interest AI and data infrastructur
 es\, and tools for governance\, oversight or digital sovereignty. PUBLIC I
 NTEREST TECH areas span sustainability and climate\, participation and civ
 ic tech\, education and literacy\, health and inclusion\, and open knowled
 ge and data. WHO THEY FUND: individuals and small teams open to open sourc
 e\, AI and iterative experimentation\, eager to combine technical capabili
 ty with societal and political awareness\, willing to share learnings\, mo
 tivated to work on pressing societal problems through technology\, with an
  early-stage but well-thought-out concept\, and eligible to work in Switze
 rland. SUPPORT: up to CHF 50\,000 in funding\, a 4-month prototyping phase
 \, coaching\, mentoring and workshops\, and access to a strong network acr
 oss tech\, policy and society. Teams are strongly encouraged (though not r
 equired) to publish code\, data or components under open-source licenses a
 nd to document their approach. TIMELINE: applications 21 July to 7 Septemb
 er 2026\; rolling jury selection August-September 2026\; prototyping mid-O
 ctober 2026 to mid-February 2027\; outputs and learnings November 2026 to 
 July 2027. Questions: info@prototypefund.ch.\n\nOrganisation: Prototype Fu
 nd Switzerland (Opendata.ch)\nLocation: Switzerland\; applicants must be e
 ligible to work in Switzerland.\nAward: Up to CHF 50\,000 per project\, pl
 us coaching\, mentoring and workshops and access to a tech/policy/society 
 network. No application fee.\nDuration: 4-month prototyping phase (mid-Oct
 ober 2026 to mid-February 2027)\n\nMore: https://prototypefund.opendata.ch
 /en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://prototypefund.opendata.ch/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Switzerland\; applicants must be eligible to work in Switzerland.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:zwillgen-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: ZwillGen Fellowship Program 2027-2028 (Technology Law)
DESCRIPTION:The ZwillGen Fellowship is a 12-month position for recent law-s
 chool graduates and early-career attorneys interested in working at the in
 tersection of law\, technology\, and public policy. Fellows work alongside
  attorneys on matters involving privacy\, cybersecurity\, artificial intel
 ligence\, online platforms\, surveillance\, digital advertising\, fintech\
 , online safety\, and technology regulation\, contributing to client couns
 eling\, investigations\, litigation\, and emerging regulatory matters\, as
  well as research\, writing\, and speaking. The program includes a reduced
  billable target (1\,250 hours) to make room for thought leadership and en
 gagement with the technology policy\, privacy\, and civil liberties commun
 ity. ELIGIBILITY: must have graduated from law school before the program s
 tarts\; must have taken or plan to take a bar exam near the start of the F
 ellowship\; must be able to work out of the DC office and be eligible to a
 pply for admission to the DC Bar. Candidates primarily interested in tradi
 tional IP practice may find it less aligned. A small number of applicants 
 may also be considered for the Hannah Schaller Memorial Skylark Fellowship
  (privacy-law focus\, with a reduced 1\,100-hour billable target for addit
 ional community engagement). Following the Fellowship\, fellows may be inv
 ited into a full-time attorney role or supported in pursuing other technol
 ogy law and policy opportunities. APPLY with a resume\, law-school transcr
 ipt\, writing sample\, and cover letter by 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation
 : ZwillGen\nLocation: Washington\, D.C.\, USA (must work out of the DC off
 ice)\nAward: Salary USD 115\,000 to USD 125\,000\, with a reduced annual b
 illable target (1\,250 hours) to create space for writing\, professional d
 evelopment\, and policy engagement. No application fee.\nDuration: 12-mont
 h position beginning Fall 2027\n\nMore: https://www.zwillgen.com/careers/f
 ellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://www.zwillgen.com/careers/fellowship-program/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, D.C.\, USA (must work out of the DC office)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:foresight-ai-science-safety-nodes-grants@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: Foresight Institute: AI for Science & Safety Nodes RFP
DESCRIPTION:Umbrella request for proposals for ambitious AI-first projects 
 that make the transition to capable AI go well and use it to unlock scienc
 e. Three connected layers\, each with its own detailed call: I. Local Comp
 ute (compute that individuals or communities physically own and control)\;
  II. Coordination and Accountability (supercollaboration and decentralized
  alignment\, human empowerment\, AI insurance and open governance)\; III. 
 AI-First Science: Bio\, Neuro\, Nano (automated nanotechnology\, whole-bra
 in emulation\, replacement\, enhancement\, biostasis). AI-first means AI i
 s the primary engine of progress\, not tapped onto an existing research pr
 ogram\; strong proposals identify narrow bottlenecks and propose something
  that can be built\, tested or measured. Selected projects receive grant f
 unding\, private local compute\, workspace at the San Francisco or Berlin 
 AI Nodes\, travel-paid field-building events and access to Foresight's adv
 isors and scientific network. ELIGIBILITY: individuals\, teams\, and nonpr
 ofit or for-profit organisations worldwide (for-profits must justify their
  need for grant funding)\; applicants who want to be active in-person cont
 ributors at the Nodes are strongly prioritised. Grants typically $30\,000 
 to $100\,000\, smaller for the Human Empowerment and AI Insurance & Open G
 overnance areas and higher for the rest. Review takes about three months a
 fter the deadline. Deadline 31 October 2026\, 23:59 PDT.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Foresight Institute\nLocation: Remote (global\; hubs in San Francisco and 
 Berlin)\nAward: Typically $30\,000 to $100\,000 per grant\, with smaller a
 mounts for the Human Empowerment and AI Insurance & Open Governance focus 
 areas and higher amounts for the rest (larger possible for the right proje
 ct)\, plus private local compute and workspace at the San Francisco or Ber
 lin AI Nodes. No application fee.\nDuration: Project-based\n\nMore: https:
 //foresight.org/grants/ai-science-safety-nodes-rfp/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://foresight.org/grants/ai-science-safety-nodes-rfp/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote (global\; hubs in San Francisco and Berlin)
END:VEVENT
