DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261123
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tarbell Fellowship 2027-28 (AI journalism)
DESCRIPTION:A year-long fellowship for journalists covering artificial inte
 lligence. Fellows complete a three-month full-time remote programme on AI 
 and journalism fundamentals\, attend a weeklong summit\, then work for nin
 e months as reporters embedded in a partner newsroom (current partners are
  in Hong Kong\, London\, Los Angeles\, New York\, San Francisco\, Seattle\
 , Washington DC and Cambridge). ELIGIBILITY: journalism or AI expertise\, 
 aged 18+ by 7 June 2027\, no degree required\; primarily for applicants wi
 th the right to work in the US\, UK or Hong Kong\, as visa sponsorship is 
 uncommon and needs partner-outlet approval.\n\nOrganisation: Tarbell Cente
 r for AI Journalism\nLocation: Remote training\, then newsroom placement i
 n the US\, UK or Hong Kong. In person.\nAward: USD 75\,000 to 120\,000 dep
 ending on experience and placement cost of living\; senior fellows (7+ yea
 rs experience) USD 115\,000 to 135\,000. Summit travel and accommodation c
 overed. No application fee.\nDuration: 12 months: remote training 7 June t
 o 6 August 2027\, in-person summit August 2027\, newsroom placement Septem
 ber 2027 to May 2028 (extendable by one year).\n\nMore: https://www.tarbel
 lcenter.org/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://www.tarbellcenter.org/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote training\, then newsroom placement in the US\, UK or Hong K
 ong. In person.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mozilla-incubator-human-scale-ai-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261120
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261121
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mozilla Foundation Incubator: Human-Scale AI cohort 2027 
 (call for proposals)
DESCRIPTION:The Mozilla Foundation Incubator's next cohort funds seven Huma
 n-Scale AI projects: working AI technologies that are small (small models\
 , less compute\, able to run locally or on low-power devices)\, intentiona
 l (users and communities get insight into and control over their data\, an
 d systems can be configured\, forked or community-governed) and tailored (
 fit-for-purpose for a specific community\, context or problem\, with parti
 cular interest in resource-constrained regions\, people with disabilities 
 and other underserved groups). Projects must already be deployed with the 
 people they serve\, even at pilot scale\, and show early product-community
  fit. Not wanted: concepts without working technology\, standalone governa
 nce or policy work\, generic chatbots\, or products whose core depends on 
 a frontier model or a slimmed-down version of one. The cohort runs for 12 
 months from April 2027 with coaching\, a Mozilla programme officer\, netwo
 rk access and a showcase at the Mozilla Festival\; required sessions are a
 bout one or two hour-long virtual meetings a month\, and teams must work i
 n the open. ELIGIBILITY: worldwide\; you do not need to be a nonprofit but
  the project must have a charitable purpose and you must be legally able t
 o receive Mozilla Foundation grant funds. Applications in English (transla
 tion tools welcome). Multi-stage process: an initial project proposal\, th
 en a full application for a shortlist. Info session 12 November 2026\, 17:
 00 UTC. DEADLINE: Friday 20 November 2026\, 11:59PM PST (UTC-8).\n\nOrgani
 sation: Mozilla Foundation (Mozilla Foundation Incubator)\nLocation: Remot
 e cohort worldwide\, with a platform at the Mozilla Festival.\nAward: USD 
 300\,000 non-dilutive funding plus 12 months of support for each of seven 
 selected projects\; projects showing strong progress may receive up to USD
  200\,000 more and two further years of support\, for a total of up to USD
  500\,000 over three years. No application fee.\nDuration: 12 months from 
 April 2027\, with possible follow-on support for two more years.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.mozillafoundation.org/en/what-we-do/grantmaking/incubator/huma
 n-scale-ai/call-for-proposals/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.mozillafoundation.org/en/what-we-do/grantmaking/incubator/h
 uman-scale-ai/call-for-proposals/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote cohort worldwide\, with a platform at the Mozilla Festival.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:foresight-ai-science-safety-nodes-rfp-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: Foresight Institute: AI for Science and Safety Nodes RFP 
 2026 (Local Compute\; Coordination and Accountability\; AI-first Science)
DESCRIPTION:Foresight Institute's AI for Science and Safety Nodes request f
 or proposals funds open\, AI-first projects across three calls: I. Local C
 ompute (compute that individuals and communities physically own and contro
 l)\, II. Coordination and Accountability (supercollaboration and decentral
 ised alignment\, human empowerment\, and AI insurance and open governance:
  multi-agent oversight and verification\, tools for collective deliberatio
 n\, decision rules for when human control adds value\, and independent ris
 k assessment or insurance mechanisms for frontier labs)\, and III. AI-firs
 t Science (automated nanotechnology\, whole-brain emulation\, frontier bio
 ). Grantees can also get workspace in the San Francisco or Berlin AI Nodes
  (a community xNode runs in Cape Town) and private compute. All funded cod
 e\, data and outputs must be open-sourced. Projects are judged on fit with
  the focus area\, impact on reducing existential risk from AI\, feasibilit
 y within 1 to 3 years\, AI-first working methods\, ability to execute\, an
 d high-risk\, high-reward potential. ELIGIBILITY: individuals\, teams and 
 organisations worldwide\; nonprofits and for-profits welcome (for-profits 
 must justify the need for grant funding). Priority goes to applicants who 
 want to work in person at one of the Nodes\, but it is not required. Revie
 w takes about three months\; applications can opt in to sharing with Light
 cone Commons. DEADLINE: 31 October 2026\, 23:59 PDT.\n\nOrganisation: Fore
 sight Institute\nLocation: Remote-friendly worldwide\; optional in-person 
 membership at the Foresight AI Nodes in San Francisco or Berlin.\nAward: G
 rants typically USD 30\,000 to 100\,000 (smaller awards for the Human Empo
 werment and AI Insurance and Open Governance areas\, larger for the rest\;
  bigger amounts possible for the right project)\, usually paid as one lump
  sum\, with up to 10 percent overhead. Optional office space in San Franci
 sco or Berlin and access to private compute clusters. No application fee.\
 nDuration: Short-term and multi-year projects\; larger multi-year grants a
 re paid in milestone tranches.\n\nMore: https://foresight.org/grants/ai-sc
 ience-safety-nodes-rfp/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://foresight.org/grants/ai-science-safety-nodes-rfp/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote-friendly worldwide\; optional in-person membership at the F
 oresight AI Nodes in San Francisco or Berlin.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:anthropic-stem-fellows-program-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261026T135004Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261028
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261029
SUMMARY:Deadline: Anthropic STEM Fellows Program 2027 (four-month AI-for-sc
 ience fellowship\, January to May 2027)
DESCRIPTION:Anthropic's four-month STEM Fellows Program brings mathematicia
 ns\, physicists\, biologists\, chemists\, engineers and other domain scien
 tists in to work on unsolved problems in their own fields using Claude. Fe
 llows join small pods of peers from related disciplines\, define hard open
  problems that Claude currently struggles with\, build datasets\, simulati
 ons and evaluations\, test how the model plans experiments and interprets 
 data\, and map where its answers are confidently wrong\, with access to fr
 ontier models and internal tools and weekly meetings with Anthropic resear
 chers. Some fellowships may be extended. ELIGIBILITY: a STEM PhD plus seve
 ral years of experience (for example postdoc or faculty)\, hands-on experi
 ence with Claude and Claude Code\, full-time availability\, and existing U
 S work authorisation (no visa sponsorship)\; prior machine-learning experi
 ence helps but is not required. Runs 12 January to 8 May 2027\, full time\
 , San Francisco by default with a required one-week in-person orientation 
 there\; remote within the US may be considered case by case. Later stages 
 include a take-home exercise and conversations with the research team. DEA
 DLINE: 28 October 2026 (no time zone stated)\; offers planned by 20 Novemb
 er 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Anthropic\nLocation: San Francisco\, United Stat
 es\, by default (one-week in-person orientation required)\; remote within 
 the US considered in some cases.\nAward: USD 100\,000 to 167\,000 for the 
 four-month fellowship (equivalent to USD 300\,000 to 500\,000 annualised)\
 , plus benefits\; fellows are engaged through an employer of record on a f
 ixed-term contract. No application fee.\nDuration: Four months full time\,
  12 January to 8 May 2027\; some fellowships may be extended.\n\nMore: htt
 ps://job-boards.greenhouse.io/anthropic/jobs/5452403008?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/anthropic/jobs/5452403008?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:San Francisco\, United States\, by default (one-week in-person ori
 entation required)\; remote within the US considered in some cases.
END:VEVENT
